fact WorkItem(id: "WI-744", description: some(value: "BUG (silent skip, at the CLI boundary): `anthill run` DEMOTES every non-blocking LoadError to `warning:` and RUNS THE PROGRAM ANYWAY, exit 0 — including `unresolved name`. This defeats the repo's \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\" principle for EVERY guard that raises the catch-all `LoadError::Other`, which is most of them.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE (2026-07-17), on the real example: take examples/classic-mini/map-colouring and annotate the relational head with sorts that DO NOT EXIST ANYWHERE — `rule colouring(?wa: NoSuchSortAnywhere, ?nt: AlsoBogus, ?sa, ?q, ?nsw, ?v) :- ...` — then `anthill run <dir>`:\n    warning: unresolved name 'NoSuchSortAnywhere' in scope 'mapcolouring' at 2288..2306\n    warning: unresolved name 'AlsoBogus' in scope 'mapcolouring' at 2313..2322\n    warning: load error: WI-582: typed rule patterns (`?x: T`) are supported only on `[simp]`/`[unfold]` ...\n    6\n    EXIT=0\nThe SAME source through `load::load_all` directly returns `Err` with those three as LOAD ERRORS. So the loader is LOUD and the CLI MUTES it. (This is what made WI-742 look like a loader/parse-convert silent skip when the loader was in fact raising exactly the error it was written to raise — see WI-742's PREMISE CORRECTION. One mis-read exit code cost a whole ticket's diagnosis.)\n\nLOCUS: rustland/anthill-cli/src/run.rs:182-195 — the `Err(errs)` arm splits per error on `e.is_load_blocking()`: blocking → `error: {e}` + `return Err(runner::EXIT_COMPILE)`; everything else → `warning: {e}` and FALL THROUGH to `Ok(kb)`. Plus rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs:679 `is_load_blocking` — an ALLOWLIST of ~25 named variants. `LoadError::Other { .. }` (load.rs, the catch-all used by dozens of deliberately-loud guards — WI-582's typed-rule-pattern guard, the multi-head-label guard, the denial-head guard, the WI-618 rule-tvar guards, ...) is ABSENT. `LoadError::UnresolvedName { .. }` (load.rs:168) is ALSO ABSENT. Both therefore demote.\n\nSEVERITY: a program with unresolved names runs to completion and reports success. Any guard whose author wrote `LoadError::Other` believing \"loud error\" got \"warning, then run\" at the only surface users actually invoke. The load-blocking allowlist is 25 variants long and every future `LoadError::Other` silently joins the muted set — the polarity means the DEFAULT for a new guard is \"advisory\", which is the opposite of the repo principle.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (decide, then do): INVERT THE POLARITY. `Err` should MEAN blocking. A genuinely advisory diagnostic already has a home — `LoadResult.warnings`, printed by run.rs:177-180 on the Ok path (the WI-346 channel, e.g. requires-shadow). So the clean shape is: every `Err` variant blocks; anything advisory MOVES to `warnings` and stops being an `Err`. That deletes `is_load_blocking` entirely rather than inverting its list, and makes the type say the thing (`Err` = will not run). The cheaper alternative — keep the predicate but make it a DENYlist (block by default, name the advisory variants) — preserves the split but keeps a footgun-shaped default; prefer the first unless the sweep below turns up many genuine advisories.\n\nREQUIRED SWEEP (this is the real work, not the 3-line change): enumerate the `LoadError::Other` and `UnresolvedName` sites and classify each as BLOCKING (nearly all — they are guards) vs ADVISORY (move to `warnings`). Also check the other CLI entry points for the same demotion (main.rs:553/570/588/1260/1313, prove.rs) — run.rs is where it was verified but it is unlikely to be the only one. Beware: existing tests/examples may currently ride the demotion (something may load with a muted error today and stop loading after this); a full-workspace run is the check, not `-p anthill-core` (WI-569 lesson).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill run` on a program with an UNRESOLVED NAME exits NONZERO and does not execute; likewise for the WI-582 typed-rule-pattern guard and the other `LoadError::Other` guards; genuinely advisory diagnostics still print and still allow the run, via the `LoadResult.warnings` channel (not via an `Err` that gets demoted); a regression test drives the CLI (or the same `Err`/exit path) over a source with an unresolved name and asserts NONZERO exit + no program output; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh (FULL workspace)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T15:07:20Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-744", author: "user", content: "SCOPE EXPANDED (user, 2026-07-17): 'can we make it errors and try to fix the underlying issues?' — the ad-hoc warn-and-proceed cluster is now fixed too, not just the LoadError demotion. These bypassed BOTH LoadError and LoadWarning (bare eprintln!(\"warning: ...\") + continue/return), so the original ticket's is_load_blocking fix did not reach them.\n\nROOT CAUSE (not a per-site symptom): collect_files_recursive returned () — NO ERROR CHANNEL — so every caller had to swallow or print. Fixed at the producer: it now takes &mut Vec<String> errors. anthill-cli's collect_anthill_files already HAD an errors vec and simply never received the dir-read failure; collect_data_files had no channel at all and now returns Result<Vec<PathBuf>, Vec<String>>. anthill-todo has its OWN duplicate copy of both (a separate finding — the two collectors are near-identical across crates); same fix applied there, collect_anthill_files -> Result.\n\nSITES CONVERTED warning -> error: anthill-cli/main.rs collect_files_recursive (unreadable dir was dropped silently -> KB missing files the user NAMED); collect_data_files (same, no channel); the .toml/.json read arm; the .toml/.json LOAD arm (the worst — genuine load errors demoted, so a query answered from a KB missing supplied data: VERIFIED a broken.toml gave 'warning: ...' + 'loaded: 2250 facts' + exit 0, now 'error: <file>: format error: TOML parse error at line 1, column 6' + exit 1). anthill-todo: collect_files_recursive + collect_anthill_files + the project-file read arm (a file we can SEE but not READ was skipped -> 'list' silently under-reported). Errors ACCUMULATE per run so one invocation reports every bad file.\n\nKEPT AS WARNINGS (deliberate, each defensible): anthill-todo main.rs:535 the PARSE arm (WI-505 — a stale domain.anthill predating a grammar change must not cascade; it is attributable and never enters the KB, and it is the ONLY site printing a proper file:line); runner.rs:77 + rust-gen bundle.rs:282 (out-of-range exit clamped — defined behavior, not a failure).\n\n-d WARNING REMOVED (user, 2026-07-17: '-d option absence when we can find anthill-todo dir is ok'): find_project_dir printed 'warning: no -d flag specified, using current directory: ...' on EVERY -d-less invocation where ./anthill-todo/ was found — i.e. on the documented normal discovery path. It guarded nothing: the message was identical whether discovery landed on the right project or the wrong one (the rustland/anthill-todo CRATE dir footgun), so it annotated everything and caught nothing. Now silent on that path; the genuinely-guessy arm (cwd itself holds .anthill files -> treat cwd AS the project) still warns, reworded to say what it is actually guessing.\n\nREVERTED (a fix that was worse than the bug): surfacing LoadResult.warnings in anthill-todo. A review agent correctly found anthill-todo DROPS merged.warnings, making WI-744's own doc ('an advisory rides LoadResult::warnings') false in that binary — same stdlib, 2 advisories under anthill run, 0 under anthill-todo. But anthill-todo embeds the stdlib AND the bundle, so those advisories concern files the user neither owns nor can fix: surfacing them put 2 requires-shadow warts on top of EVERY anthill-todo command. cmd_default_parity_test's golden transcript caught it. The drop is deliberate and now documented at the site; the residual cost (an advisory about the user's OWN project file is dropped too) needs per-file attribution -> WI-745.\n\nNEW TESTS: anthill-cli/tests/load_cmd_test.rs (+ fixtures/load/{broken-data,good-data}) — a broken .toml blocks with a loud error naming the file; the same source without it loads (the control, so the failure is attributable to the data file). NOT covered: the unreadable-DIRECTORY arm — provoking it needs a chmod-000 dir, a no-op under a root runner and flaky in CI; recorded in the test's header.", at: "2026-07-17T12:30:00Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-744", author: "user", content: "VERIFY PASS COMPLETE (2026-07-17), delivering. The adversarial verify + gap sweep (agents died on 529 overload; both threads finished by hand) surfaced two more real issues, now fixed:\n\n1. SNIFFER FALSE-POSITIVE (same class as the Cargo.toml regression, rarer shape). is_anthill_data keyed on a bare 'meta' key, so a foreign TOML with [meta]/[data] sections and a JSON with a nested 'meta' object were claimed and errored (VERIFIED: both blocked the load, exit 1). Retightened to key on meta.entity being a STRING — exactly what load_section requires, and what every real data file carries — so no loadable file is rejected. Both now load clean. Fixtures foreign-files/{config.toml,manifest.json} pin it. Residual (now a false-NEGATIVE: a genuine-but-entity-less data file skipped silently) is inherent to sniff-by-shape and recorded on WI-746.\n\n2. FALSE COMMENT on anthill-todo collect_anthill_files: the '!path.exists() { continue; }' skip was justified by 'scan_dir points at a dir that does not exist yet' — fiction (scan_dir only ever returns an existing dir; find_project_dir proves it). Removed the skip; a non-directory is now a loud error. Also fixed the load-arm asymmetry comment, which implied the parse arm skips because it is ATTRIBUTABLE — it skips because of WI-505 REDUNDANCY (bundle supplies the stale file's defs). The read arm is attributable too and correctly BLOCKS.\n\nVERIFIED the challenged read-arm block is correct: traced by hand that the old warning+continue does NOT hit the record_source zip corruption (that was unique to the load_all_per_file empty-Vec arm); the block stands on its own ground (unreadable file = unknown contents = silent under-report if skipped).\n\nFINAL DELIVERED SCOPE (grew from the original 3-line CLI fix):\n  CORE: deleted is_load_blocking allowlist -> every LoadError blocks; advisory rides LoadResult.warnings. LoadError::Other Display drops its 'load error: ' prefix (was doubling). UnresolvedName/UnresolvedImport/AmbiguousSymbol got the blocking rationale the deleted doc held.\n  CLI (run/main/todo): all three Err arms print error: + exit. collect_queries scan_definitions demotion fixed (anthill query blocked on a typo'd import). The ad-hoc warn-and-continue CLUSTER converted to errors at the PRODUCER (collect_files_recursive gained an errors channel); collect_data_files -> Result; .flatten() drop fixed in both collectors (per-entry io errors were dropped). Data files sniffed for the meta.entity envelope so foreign .toml/.json (Cargo.toml, package.json, JSONC .vscode/settings.json) are ignored not fatal. anthill-todo discovery by MARKER (project.anthill/workitems.anthill) not by dir-name, closing the rustland/anthill-todo crate-dir footgun. -d-less discovery no longer warns on the normal path.\n  TESTS: run_cmd_test (4 new: 3 promoted variants block + advisory still runs), load_cmd_test (broken-data blocks, foreign-files ignored, control), query_cmd_test (3: bad import blocks, control, bare pattern), shared tests/common/mod.rs harness with line-wise has_diagnostic. wi440 test renamed. Full workspace 2960 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh.\n  FILED: WI-745 (no file:line on load diagnostics), WI-746 (data files opt-in vs slurp), WI-747 (8 collector copies), WI-748 (todo init ignores -d). NOT committed.", at: "2026-07-17T15:07:18Z")
